  "notes": "The bass-player half of the Cheiron/Maratone board: 84–110 straight, backbeat on 2&4, C minor with i–VI–III–VII on the 1998 debut he co-produced and a pre-chorus that \"hangs on VI or iv before dropping back to i\" — both authored as families. The drum claim that separates him from the rest of the board is a relationship rather than a placement: \"a kick that **answers the vocal's rhythmic gaps**\", which is `syncopation` at 0.5, the loosest kick on the Cheiron bench. The other two deltas are quoted: \"hats 8ths in verse, 16ths from the pre-chorus\" (an evenly weighted 8th/16th base) and the fill vocabulary — \"16th snare roll or reversed crash\" — which is the snare-roll ladder plus a `reverse` lane. The bass is the entry's point: \"**he came up as a bassist** (from age 13)… the bass lines are played-feeling even when synthesised: root-and-octave 8ths with a slide into section changes, and a real electric bass doubling the sub\", which is `independent_riff` at 0.9 with a real `glideProb` and the octave as a passing tone. The melody is \"kept inside a narrow octave so it works for a young voice\", which is the 67–79 register, and \"the hook is repeated three times per chorus\" is the two-bar phrase weighting. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability and `bpm.mode` 95.",
